{"number": 93, "text": "                       Wasn't        it                      Are     you     crazy\n                       Questions          (2)\n\n                       Questions are often used to make a comment or exclamation rather than to find out or check\n                       information. An answer is not necessarily expected.\n\n                       Negative yes—no questions are commonly used to show surprise, pleasure, etc., particularly when\n                       we encourage others to agree with us. These usually have a falling tone:\n                             WASn’t  it TERrible ?                 DIDn’t she sound riDICulous\n                             HAVen’t  | been STUpid                         they BEAUtiful\n\n                       In informal English we can also use positive yes—no questions, particularly to express criticism.\n                      These  often have a rising tone:\n                             Are you CRAzy  @?                     Have you gone MAD    @?\n\n                       Wh- questions are also used to make a comment, particularly ones with modal verbs. These\n                       usually have a falling tone:\n                             HOW    was I supposed to know         WHAT?’S  it to do with YOU  @?\n                }      Sometimes we  ask a question and suggest a possible answer ourselves before the hearer replies.\n                       As the purpose of these possible answers is to make sure that what we think we know is correct,\n                       they usually have a fall-rising tone (see Unit 43):\n                             Where  are you off to? rhe SUpermarket\n                             How  are you getting there? with NICola\n                             What  are you going to wear? NOT  that old JUMper again  &   2?\n\n                                                                                  Not that old\n                                                                                  jumper again?\n\n\n                  C78 We  can use questions to give instructions or make suggestions. These usually have a falling tone,\n                       and often (but not always) include modal verbs:\n                             will you PLEASE  leave it aLONE            COULD    we have the BILL, please &?\n                             WOULD    you turn the LIGHT  off           COULDn’t   you just reFUSE &@?\n                             WHY   don’t you go by TRAIN                HOW    about putting it over THERE\n\n\n              94          English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 2567, "mean_conf": 89.22, "word_count": 292, "words": [{"text": "Wasn't", "x0": 460, "y0": 109, "x1": 714, "y1": 173, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "it", "x0": 750, "y0": 110, "x1": 797, "y1": 173, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "terrible?", "x0": 829, "y0": 108, "x1": 1210, "y1": 174, "conf": 40, "bold": false}, {"text": "Are", "x0": 1241, "y0": 109, "x1": 1363, "y1": 173, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "you", "x0": 1394, "y0": 128, "x1": 1522, "y1": 192, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "crazy", "x0": 1559, "y0": 109, "x1": 1852, "y1": 192, "conf": 81, "bold": false}, {"text": "&?", "x0": 1774, "y0": 104, "x1": 1852, "y1": 196, "conf": 8, "bold": false}, {"text": "Questions", "x0": 460, "y0": 202, "x1": 821, "y1": 267, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "(2)", "x0": 860, "y0": 199, "x1": 951, "y1": 281, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Questions", "x0": 459, "y0": 348, "x1": 646, "y1": 391, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "are", "x0": 662, "y0": 359, "x1": 717, "y1": 381, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "often", "x0": 735, "y0": 346, "x1": 825, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "used", "x0": 846, "y0": 346, "x1": 923, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "to", "x0": 942, "y0": 356, "x1": 977, "y1": 381, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "make", "x0": 996, "y0": 346,